WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.800
Membrane current measured across a whole cell was thought to be the sum of many

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.060
small pores opening and closing.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.820
The steps of one pore were too small to see against the noise of a large membrane.

4
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.800
One open channel passes a few picoamps, a few trillionths of an amp.

5
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.800
Until single-channel recording, what one channel does had to be inferred from

6
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.260
thousands acting at once.

7
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:37.633
A glass pipette, with a tip about a micrometre across, is pressed against a cell and

8
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:39.853
sealed to the membrane with slight suction.

9
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:45.893
The seal must resist above a gigohm, so current flows through the patch and not

10
00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:52.333
around the rim. Neher and Sakmann's earliest seals were about 10 to 20 megohms, and

11
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:54.893
leak around the rim buried the signal in noise.

12
00:00:55.643 --> 00:01:01.483
The 1981 paper describes seals of a billion to a hundred billion ohms,

13
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:02.193
called giga-seals.

14
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:09.077
An amplifier holds the pipette at a voltage you choose and records the current that

15
00:01:09.077 --> 00:01:11.557
flows. That is voltage clamp.

16
00:01:12.557 --> 00:01:15.917
In the cell-attached form, the patch stays on the cell.

17
00:01:16.757 --> 00:01:21.517
With an agonist in the pipette, one channel's openings appear as square steps.

18
00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:27.557
Pull the patch off with its cytoplasmic face exposed to the bath, and you have an

19
00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:28.717
inside-out patch.

20
00:01:29.457 --> 00:01:31.257
You can change the solution on that face.

21
00:01:32.477 --> 00:01:37.997
Start from whole-cell mode and pull away instead, and the membrane reseals with its

22
00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:41.337
extracellular face to the bath: an outside-out patch.

23
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.920
In whole-cell mode, the pipette gains access to the cell interior, so ions and small

24
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.860
molecules exchange between the pipette solution and the cytoplasm.

25
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.740
Perforated patch uses a pore-former, gramicidin or nystatin, for electrical access

26
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.540
while the cell keeps most of its own contents.

27
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.400
In current clamp you set the injected current and record the voltage.

28
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.720
A current step gives a voltage response.

29
00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.880
The steady voltage change, divided by the current, is the input resistance.

30
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.020
The charging curve gives the time constant.

31
00:02:21.833 --> 00:02:28.433
The trace sits at zero, steps to a fixed level and steps back as one channel opens

32
00:02:28.433 --> 00:02:32.413
and shuts. The step height is the unitary current.

33
00:02:33.293 --> 00:02:36.913
The fraction of time the channel is open is the open probability.

34
00:02:38.013 --> 00:02:44.153
In this idealised example, the open level is −2 picoamps, open 200

35
00:02:44.153 --> 00:02:45.673
milliseconds of one second.

36
00:02:46.513 --> 00:02:48.673
Open probability is 0.2.

37
00:02:49.513 --> 00:02:54.733
After a drug, the level is the same, but the channel is open 500 milliseconds a

38
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:57.773
second. Open probability is 0.5.

39
00:02:58.713 --> 00:03:01.173
The drug changed gating, not conduction.

40
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:07.747
Brief closures shorter than the recording can resolve vanish from the trace, so

41
00:03:07.747 --> 00:03:10.147
report the filter and the sampling rate.

42
00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:16.107
And a conventional whole-cell pipette replaces the cell's chloride, so the GABA

43
00:03:16.107 --> 00:03:19.167
reversal potential reflects the experimenter's recipe.

44
00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:23.607
Gramicidin perforated patch leaves the cell's chloride alone.

45
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:30.110
Neher and Sakmann recorded single-channel currents from denervated frog muscle in

46
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:36.110
1976. Their records showed square steps from one channel opening and shutting.

47
00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:44.450
In 1981 Hamill, Marty, Neher, Sakmann and Sigworth described the gigaseal methods,

48
00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.830
including excised patches and whole-cell recording.

49
00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:56.743
In 1985 Rorsman and Trube found that 20 mM glucose suppressed potassium

50
00:03:56.743 --> 00:04:01.443
channel activity in cell-attached patches of cultured mouse beta cells.

51
00:04:02.503 --> 00:04:09.023
3 mM ATP on the cytoplasmic face of excised inside-out patches reversibly

52
00:04:09.023 --> 00:04:10.063
blocked the channels.

53
00:04:11.063 --> 00:04:14.703
The excised patch let them test a metabolite on the inner face.

54
00:04:15.703 --> 00:04:23.783
In 1989 Derkach, Surprenant and North recorded serotonin-evoked unitary currents in

55
00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:27.063
outside-out patches from guinea-pig enteric neurons.

56
00:04:28.403 --> 00:04:33.723
Serotonin opening single channels in patches with no cytoplasm was evidence that the

57
00:04:33.723 --> 00:04:36.803
5-HT3 receptor is itself a channel.

58
00:04:37.523 --> 00:04:43.343
Keep this rule. A current from cultured cells, or from cells engineered to make the

59
00:04:43.343 --> 00:04:47.863
channel, belongs to that preparation and protocol, not to a living animal.
